    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2737\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to direct the commissioner of education to recalculate certain\nstate aid payments which may be payable to the Marlboro central school\ndistrict; and to establish a 30 year period of probable usefulness for\nthe payment of certain judgments by such school district\n\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe Marlboro Central School District includes the Danskammer and\nRoseton power plants. The established assessed real property value for\nthese power plants have made the Marlboro Central School District\nappear to be very wealthy, subjecting the school district to reduced\nstate aid as a result.\n\nAs has occurred in other parts of the State, after these power plants\nwere sold by public utilities to private power producing consortiums,\nsuch new owners moved to significantly reduce (by approximately 90%)\nthe assessed value of both plants. The power plant owners have\nsucceeded in their tax certiorari proceedings. This means that past\noverpayments will require reimbursement by the taxing jurisdictions in\nwhich Danskammer and Roseton power plants are located. The\noverpayments may go back as far as fiscal year 2003.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Adds a new unconsolidated law to allow for the filing of a\ncourt order or judgment pursuant to Education Law section 3604(5)(b).\nIt allows for transmitting revised data to the Commissioner of\nEducation pursuant to Education Law section 3603(1)(c) and\ncertification of payments due as a result of a court order or\njudgment.\n\nWhat this bill does is allow the school district to go back to that\nsame period of time of reassessment and reconfigure the school aid for\nthe Marlboro School District for the period of scheduled school\nproperty tax reimbursements to the power plant owners. The bill\nrecognizes that the power plants have not been as much of an asset in\nprevious years as was originally determined by Education Department in\nformulating state aid payments to the school district.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nNone, but similar provisions have been enacted for the\nHaverstraw-Stoney Point School District in relation to power plants\nlocated in that jurisdiction.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis local school district needs to be made whole if the real property\ntax assessment is substantially reduced. Otherwise, real property tax\nassessments will increase rapidly for the taxpayers of this\njurisdiction. The bill also allows the School District to bond no more\nthan $ 11 million to spread out the cost of this new obligation.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nS.4497-B of 2008\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n LOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nTo be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediate.\n",
